
<topic id="E65CEF7346695ADB9A5BDCD95B085772" revisionNumber="1">

<developerConceptualDocument xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

<introduction>

<para>
Previous section: <link xlink:href="E771D4FEB3C4838C0A284850BEEFBF46">Retardation fit functions</link></para>
<autoOutline />
<markup><hr/></markup></introduction>

<section address="B842FB1B6E4DB3EAEC1F5A6ACBE05B24">
<title>Arruda-Boyce 8-chain model (biaxial loading)
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the biaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#EFE158BE46897C009D3262F40A2815A9"><img src="../media/EFE158BE46897C009D3262F40A2815A9.png" width="187" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with the stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B"><img src="../media/9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> : rubber-elastic (entropic) shear modulus of the equivalent Gaussian network μ = n·k·Θ, with n: number of chains per unit volume (chain density), k: Boltzmann constant and Θ: absolute temperature in K
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : maximum value of the stretch (parameter 2 of the fit function 'LambdaM')
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2C151232A85576132AEA78F9683BFC3A"><img src="../media/2C151232A85576132AEA78F9683BFC3A.png" width="97" height="20" align="texttop"></img></a></markup> is the stretch of each chain in the model
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#636712DB8B0AD3D429CC99C270C64D69"><img src="../media/636712DB8B0AD3D429CC99C270C64D69.png" width="36" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> is the inverse Langevin function
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FB8859F25D28AD6A1184B90A9F67FA6D"><img src="../media/FB8859F25D28AD6A1184B90A9F67FA6D.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> is the first stretch invariant, which is for the biaxial case <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1804D4DA50B5F676D56C2BBA0C0BE9A1"><img src="../media/1804D4DA50B5F676D56C2BBA0C0BE9A1.png" width="120" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
Note that, depending on <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, the shear modulus G is slightly higher than <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B"><img src="../media/9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>, because of the following relationship: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9240B022B8E8532D29A05DABDB85CB3B"><img src="../media/9240B022B8E8532D29A05DABDB85CB3B.png" width="134" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#88CF5B28A422173AAF5D88EED8376A62"><img src="../media/88CF5B28A422173AAF5D88EED8376A62.png" width="121" height="16" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>. Note that therefore the parameter <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> must be greater than 1 + the greatest strain value in your data.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#06D1C392C192F42E994E7D7B95B0AC9F"><img src="../media/06D1C392C192F42E994E7D7B95B0AC9F.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's biaxial data (black circles) fitted with the Arruda-Boyce 8-chain model for the biaxial case (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#25AA2615F4D2CE14DD2962D15B823DF2"><img src="../media/25AA2615F4D2CE14DD2962D15B823DF2.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FC49C9CFA84258F7E3690F71AD9596BD"><img src="../media/FC49C9CFA84258F7E3690F71AD9596BD.png" width="73" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA76A5D167CA8529ECF7BC6B326B49C4"><img src="../media/BA76A5D167CA8529ECF7BC6B326B49C4.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial data [2], and a fit with the Arruda-Boyce 8-chain models for equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial load simultaneously to all three data sets using the same parameters. The resulting fit parameter is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4F98AD1E7510C0CD734C608C1674FFD1"><img src="../media/4F98AD1E7510C0CD734C608C1674FFD1.png" width="97" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#EA91E16012DCEBF738EB37282513085D"><img src="../media/EA91E16012DCEBF738EB37282513085D.png" width="58" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> (fixed by lower boundary).
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
E. M. Arruda and M. C. Boyce, “A three-dimensional constitutive model for the large stretch behavior of rubber elastic materials,” Journal of the Mechanics and Physics of Solids, vol. 41, no. 2, pp. 389–412, Feb. 1993, doi: 10.1016/0022-5096(93)90013-6.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="3B1AB8AABB4CE1DA6B8EA166C817217E">
<title>Arruda-Boyce 8-chain model (planar loading)
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the planar stretching (width&gt;&gt;length) of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#EFE158BE46897C009D3262F40A2815A9"><img src="../media/EFE158BE46897C009D3262F40A2815A9.png" width="187" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with the stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B"><img src="../media/9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> :  rubber-elastic (entropic) shear modulus of the equivalent Gaussian network μ = n·k·Θ, with n: number of chains per unit volume (chain density), k: Boltzmann constant and Θ: absolute temperature in K
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : maximum value of the stretch (parameter 2 of the fit function 'LambdaM')
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2C151232A85576132AEA78F9683BFC3A"><img src="../media/2C151232A85576132AEA78F9683BFC3A.png" width="97" height="20" align="texttop"></img></a></markup> is the stretch of each chain in the model
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#636712DB8B0AD3D429CC99C270C64D69"><img src="../media/636712DB8B0AD3D429CC99C270C64D69.png" width="36" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> is the inverse Langevin function
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FB8859F25D28AD6A1184B90A9F67FA6D"><img src="../media/FB8859F25D28AD6A1184B90A9F67FA6D.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> is the first stretch invariant, which is for the planar case <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6F00E0EB56C268463C4722089B55D023"><img src="../media/6F00E0EB56C268463C4722089B55D023.png" width="102" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
Note that, depending on <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, the shear modulus G is slightly higher than <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B"><img src="../media/9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>, because of the following relationship: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9240B022B8E8532D29A05DABDB85CB3B"><img src="../media/9240B022B8E8532D29A05DABDB85CB3B.png" width="134" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#88CF5B28A422173AAF5D88EED8376A62"><img src="../media/88CF5B28A422173AAF5D88EED8376A62.png" width="121" height="16" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>. Note that therefore the parameter <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> must be greater than 1 + the greatest strain value in your data.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#63509DF5D1B7E6FC785153C87CB73A05"><img src="../media/63509DF5D1B7E6FC785153C87CB73A05.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's pure shear data (black circles) fitted with the Arruda-Boyce 8-chain model for the planar case (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A9775E46975DEC3C04213E477E0AF542"><img src="../media/A9775E46975DEC3C04213E477E0AF542.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#60BE0938429945379E6E5FCDE4D8509F"><img src="../media/60BE0938429945379E6E5FCDE4D8509F.png" width="73" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
E. M. Arruda and M. C. Boyce, “A three-dimensional constitutive model for the large stretch behavior of rubber elastic materials,” Journal of the Mechanics and Physics of Solids, vol. 41, no. 2, pp. 389–412, Feb. 1993, doi: 10.1016/0022-5096(93)90013-6.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="0401BAE10043E7921E9EEB0456AD8182">
<title>Arruda-Boyce 8-chain model (uniaxial loading)
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> as a function of engineering strain <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> during the uniaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#EFE158BE46897C009D3262F40A2815A9"><img src="../media/EFE158BE46897C009D3262F40A2815A9.png" width="187" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with the stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B"><img src="../media/9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> :  rubber-elastic (entropic) shear modulus of the equivalent Gaussian network μ = n·k·Θ, with n: number of chains per unit volume (chain density), k: Boltzmann constant and Θ: absolute temperature in K
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : maximum value of the stretch (parameter 2 of the fit function 'LambdaM')
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2C151232A85576132AEA78F9683BFC3A"><img src="../media/2C151232A85576132AEA78F9683BFC3A.png" width="97" height="20" align="texttop"></img></a></markup> is the stretch of each chain in the model
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#636712DB8B0AD3D429CC99C270C64D69"><img src="../media/636712DB8B0AD3D429CC99C270C64D69.png" width="36" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> is the inverse Langevin function
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FB8859F25D28AD6A1184B90A9F67FA6D"><img src="../media/FB8859F25D28AD6A1184B90A9F67FA6D.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> is the first stretch invariant, which is for the uniaxial case <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12A24996030CC954E0B32A62CEDCE51D"><img src="../media/12A24996030CC954E0B32A62CEDCE51D.png" width="89" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
Note that, depending on <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, the shear modulus G is slightly higher than <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B"><img src="../media/9A2F7B36142BEBEAE113155CF791349B.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>, because of the following relationship: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9240B022B8E8532D29A05DABDB85CB3B"><img src="../media/9240B022B8E8532D29A05DABDB85CB3B.png" width="134" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#88CF5B28A422173AAF5D88EED8376A62"><img src="../media/88CF5B28A422173AAF5D88EED8376A62.png" width="121" height="16" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>. Note that therefore the parameter <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871"><img src="../media/3216279B3E268DACB480975462160871.png" width="18" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> must be greater than 1 + the greatest strain value in your data.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#27A975877E91915BEA01CFEE4E507A50"><img src="../media/27A975877E91915BEA01CFEE4E507A50.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's uniaxial data (black circles) fitted with the Arruda-Boyce 8-chain model for the unaxial case (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DC4286D007705EC5809F48B52D88DCC1"><img src="../media/DC4286D007705EC5809F48B52D88DCC1.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0B94684EF000627447AD0A91EC64FC10"><img src="../media/0B94684EF000627447AD0A91EC64FC10.png" width="80" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
E. M. Arruda and M. C. Boyce, “A three-dimensional constitutive model for the large stretch behavior of rubber elastic materials,” Journal of the Mechanics and Physics of Solids, vol. 41, no. 2, pp. 389–412, Feb. 1993, doi: 10.1016/0022-5096(93)90013-6.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="8BC466FC8D2ECBB0ED1EABF77E3C11C7">
<title>Bergström-Boyce model (uniaxial, equi-biaxial and planar loading)
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#09DC6ABA83CADFABA3278C50E176EBDF"><img src="../media/09DC6ABA83CADFABA3278C50E176EBDF.png" width="25" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup> or the Cauchy stress <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> as a function of engineering strain <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> during the stretching of an elastomeric material by assuming a hyperelastic spring (network A) in parallel with network B consisting of a hyperelastic spring and a dashpot.
</para>

<para>
The principal stretches for the different loading cases are:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A485FFE74D2699C4A69E6E0683831DF4"><img src="../media/A485FFE74D2699C4A69E6E0683831DF4.png" width="202" height="88"></img></a></markup>

<para>
The strain stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#33CE90416B8FFAB700B768AAE51B687C"><img src="../media/33CE90416B8FFAB700B768AAE51B687C.png" width="9" height="16" align="texttop"></img></a></markup> calculates to:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0201772A29D5A22C26995DF444735319"><img src="../media/0201772A29D5A22C26995DF444735319.png" width="192" height="45"></img></a></markup>

<para>
Network A and network B are each represented by their own 8-chain network and may have distinct limiting chain stretches <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#03570878309DB2383B5DEA333A446B85"><img src="../media/03570878309DB2383B5DEA333A446B85.png" width="27" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FBE1E3BDB066800ED6507360EBF3872F"><img src="../media/FBE1E3BDB066800ED6507360EBF3872F.png" width="27" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
For network A, the stress <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9142DAC33BE9DC53ACF81B3BE10E850E"><img src="../media/9142DAC33BE9DC53ACF81B3BE10E850E.png" width="17" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> is:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F337ECEFCD3CB8CE4AEA940DB6127F47"><img src="../media/F337ECEFCD3CB8CE4AEA940DB6127F47.png" width="193" height="41"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1C5C40FBA6C4BB854433D16707127693"><img src="../media/1C5C40FBA6C4BB854433D16707127693.png" width="24" height="16" align="texttop"></img></a></markup> being the inverse Langevin function, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#EE08375A8870F8D3299AC5649339033B"><img src="../media/EE08375A8870F8D3299AC5649339033B.png" width="9" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> being the shear modulus (parameter) and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#03570878309DB2383B5DEA333A446B85"><img src="../media/03570878309DB2383B5DEA333A446B85.png" width="27" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup> being the locking stretch of network A.
</para>

<para>
For network B, the total stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#8B9284C3023EBA66F35D6D35477DEA6A"><img src="../media/8B9284C3023EBA66F35D6D35477DEA6A.png" width="13" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> is the product of the elastic (partially) stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B9BC26E7D75975123FA2F64683C653A6"><img src="../media/B9BC26E7D75975123FA2F64683C653A6.png" width="14" height="15" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> and the viscous stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FC948DA6D200777322125B0B703B91DE"><img src="../media/FC948DA6D200777322125B0B703B91DE.png" width="14" height="15" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F75A96A3DA22A5BBF88A3F1D9FF92E97"><img src="../media/F75A96A3DA22A5BBF88A3F1D9FF92E97.png" width="129" height="15"></img></a></markup>

<para>
and the deviatoric stresses are:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#057AE5E85B9A66B246A286530EB10C5B"><img src="../media/057AE5E85B9A66B246A286530EB10C5B.png" width="308" height="41"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6DD17BD892E7CB9754C8FAFEEA4960A2"><img src="../media/6DD17BD892E7CB9754C8FAFEEA4960A2.png" width="7" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> being a parameter, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FBE1E3BDB066800ED6507360EBF3872F"><img src="../media/FBE1E3BDB066800ED6507360EBF3872F.png" width="27" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup> being the locking stretch of network B and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6A5AFEAE7586996A1543D88C324A6F90"><img src="../media/6A5AFEAE7586996A1543D88C324A6F90.png" width="14" height="16" align="texttop"></img></a></markup> being the effective partially elastic stretch of network B.
</para>

<para>
The effective (driving) flow stress then is:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4B798DFFE9F24EB432577EE5563E62AB"><img src="../media/4B798DFFE9F24EB432577EE5563E62AB.png" width="187" height="34"></img></a></markup>

<para>
and the flow rate:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#957ACF582255FB70500D87B97B4EC413"><img src="../media/957ACF582255FB70500D87B97B4EC413.png" width="198" height="29"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#75064C27E3D052F8F27E8C520763AE64"><img src="../media/75064C27E3D052F8F27E8C520763AE64.png" width="7" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A6DDC8207DD2A5A65F1233DC000F2E53"><img src="../media/A6DDC8207DD2A5A65F1233DC000F2E53.png" width="11" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#82E0FD027FF2CD5212AE87D76007C685"><img src="../media/82E0FD027FF2CD5212AE87D76007C685.png" width="8" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1011903A76A9C4D8440B87783040B11E"><img src="../media/1011903A76A9C4D8440B87783040B11E.png" width="22" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2C3A6651FE3DC1F1594E7ADEC315B14B"><img src="../media/2C3A6651FE3DC1F1594E7ADEC315B14B.png" width="13" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> being parameters.
</para>

<para>
The evolution of <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#438AABCB5E388E2385CE14D23876CFE7"><img src="../media/438AABCB5E388E2385CE14D23876CFE7.png" width="14" height="15" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> then results from:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9B34FA0DEFC5698925965F3DA2968054"><img src="../media/9B34FA0DEFC5698925965F3DA2968054.png" width="107" height="38"></img></a></markup>

<para>
Note: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6ECE3DBB5E9C8AA1D707F3FD5AB85DF9"><img src="../media/6ECE3DBB5E9C8AA1D707F3FD5AB85DF9.png" width="14" height="16" align="texttop"></img></a></markup> is calculated in the same way as <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#33CE90416B8FFAB700B768AAE51B687C"><img src="../media/33CE90416B8FFAB700B768AAE51B687C.png" width="9" height="16" align="texttop"></img></a></markup>, but with the triple <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DF4CCA4161DF478FAF8D2326FB85A99B"><img src="../media/DF4CCA4161DF478FAF8D2326FB85A99B.png" width="56" height="15" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>, that results from <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#438AABCB5E388E2385CE14D23876CFE7"><img src="../media/438AABCB5E388E2385CE14D23876CFE7.png" width="14" height="15" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> from the same formulas as <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6FE88CD61A35AED4E6FB1A7C740EE423"><img src="../media/6FE88CD61A35AED4E6FB1A7C740EE423.png" width="56" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> above for  the different loading modes (symmetry assumption of the flow).
</para>

<para>
The stress of network B then calculates to:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A945BC4020340A9BE1E1CBE5DD6D4C7F"><img src="../media/A945BC4020340A9BE1E1CBE5DD6D4C7F.png" width="89" height="17"></img></a></markup>

<para>
and the total stress is:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#15325117F2F7795C0241DD17DFE87470"><img src="../media/15325117F2F7795C0241DD17DFE87470.png" width="81" height="13"></img></a></markup>

<para>
The engineering stress <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#09DC6ABA83CADFABA3278C50E176EBDF"><img src="../media/09DC6ABA83CADFABA3278C50E176EBDF.png" width="25" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup> that is provided by the models as dependent variable then calculates to:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#8BB35A0C4D0D877686F408992F7DC1BE"><img src="../media/8BB35A0C4D0D877686F408992F7DC1BE.png" width="59" height="34"></img></a></markup>

<para>
<legacyBold>Parametrizations</legacyBold>
</para>

<para>
The model supports three different parametrizations, selected via the Parametrization property. They differ in how many raw fit parameters are exposed and how these map to the internal quantities <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B87D21C13D9F353F19EEB5752D1C7C2F"><img src="../media/B87D21C13D9F353F19EEB5752D1C7C2F.png" width="183" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup> used in the equations above.
</para>

<para>
<legacyItalic><legacyBold>PolyUMod (8 parameters, shared limiting stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4A1624075207977EFC0236E13BA97F5A"><img src="../media/4A1624075207977EFC0236E13BA97F5A.png" width="110" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>):</legacyBold></legacyItalic>
</para>

<table>

<tableHeader>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
Index
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
Name
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
Meaning
</para>
</entry></row>
</tableHeader>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
0
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
Mu
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#EE08375A8870F8D3299AC5649339033B"><img src="../media/EE08375A8870F8D3299AC5649339033B.png" width="9" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> — shear modulus of network A
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
1
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
LambdaL
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A810913FDEA8B020901F8D15D59FCCD8"><img src="../media/A810913FDEA8B020901F8D15D59FCCD8.png" width="16" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — limiting chain stretch, shared by network A and B (<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4A1624075207977EFC0236E13BA97F5A"><img src="../media/4A1624075207977EFC0236E13BA97F5A.png" width="110" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>)
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
2
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
S
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6DD17BD892E7CB9754C8FAFEEA4960A2"><img src="../media/6DD17BD892E7CB9754C8FAFEEA4960A2.png" width="7" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — relative shear modulus of network B, i.e. <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FE8584523E790D6234825A8FBC3CEFBE"><img src="../media/FE8584523E790D6234825A8FBC3CEFBE.png" width="56" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
3
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
Xi
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#75064C27E3D052F8F27E8C520763AE64"><img src="../media/75064C27E3D052F8F27E8C520763AE64.png" width="7" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — regularization near <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E89240AB2AD0AC489CE4475280452245"><img src="../media/E89240AB2AD0AC489CE4475280452245.png" width="45" height="15" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> in the flow law
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
4
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
C
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
strain exponent in the flow law (typically negative)
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
5
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
TauBase
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#82E0FD027FF2CD5212AE87D76007C685"><img src="../media/82E0FD027FF2CD5212AE87D76007C685.png" width="8" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — flow resistance, in pascals
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
6
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
M
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2C3A6651FE3DC1F1594E7ADEC315B14B"><img src="../media/2C3A6651FE3DC1F1594E7ADEC315B14B.png" width="13" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — stress exponent in the flow law
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
7
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
TauCut
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1011903A76A9C4D8440B87783040B11E"><img src="../media/1011903A76A9C4D8440B87783040B11E.png" width="22" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> — normalized threshold stress for flow
</para>
</entry></row>
</table>

<para>
Converted internally as: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2CF458DF5B198281043B0DDCCB7D20D7"><img src="../media/2CF458DF5B198281043B0DDCCB7D20D7.png" width="63" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>,  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#68A15A6F1396630C9586639210BCD88A"><img src="../media/68A15A6F1396630C9586639210BCD88A.png" width="63" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<legacyItalic><legacyBold>Ansys (7 parameters, independent limiting stretches <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4C8E0EB7660C3DB5B98A89BB404D0EC7"><img src="../media/4C8E0EB7660C3DB5B98A89BB404D0EC7.png" width="74" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup> possible; no  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#75064C27E3D052F8F27E8C520763AE64"><img src="../media/75064C27E3D052F8F27E8C520763AE64.png" width="7" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, no  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1011903A76A9C4D8440B87783040B11E"><img src="../media/1011903A76A9C4D8440B87783040B11E.png" width="22" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>):</legacyBold></legacyItalic>
</para>

