M
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2C3A6651FE3DC1F1594E7ADEC315B14B"><img src="../media/2C3A6651FE3DC1F1594E7ADEC315B14B.png" width="13" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — stress exponent in the flow law
</para>
</entry></row>
</table>

<para>
Converted internally as: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A2C5056550B90F4CC453EE561D5AC4DF"><img src="../media/A2C5056550B90F4CC453EE561D5AC4DF.png" width="46" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6F0C1353077251607F6091C5B99157E8"><img src="../media/6F0C1353077251607F6091C5B99157E8.png" width="69" height="16" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2ACC05C873BB49EA3BE06036A7CC7883"><img src="../media/2ACC05C873BB49EA3BE06036A7CC7883.png" width="34" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B40D5833D4CEBD09B7BE95DF99CC3974"><img src="../media/B40D5833D4CEBD09B7BE95DF99CC3974.png" width="49" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<legacyItalic><legacyBold>Abaqus (7 parameters, shared limiting stretch <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4A1624075207977EFC0236E13BA97F5A"><img src="../media/4A1624075207977EFC0236E13BA97F5A.png" width="110" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>; no <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1011903A76A9C4D8440B87783040B11E"><img src="../media/1011903A76A9C4D8440B87783040B11E.png" width="22" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>):</legacyBold></legacyItalic>
</para>

<table>

<tableHeader>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
Index
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
Name
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
Meaning
</para>
</entry></row>
</tableHeader>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
0
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
Mu
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#EE08375A8870F8D3299AC5649339033B"><img src="../media/EE08375A8870F8D3299AC5649339033B.png" width="9" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> — shear modulus of network A
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
1
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
LambdaL
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A810913FDEA8B020901F8D15D59FCCD8"><img src="../media/A810913FDEA8B020901F8D15D59FCCD8.png" width="16" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — limiting chain stretch, shared by network A and B (<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4A1624075207977EFC0236E13BA97F5A"><img src="../media/4A1624075207977EFC0236E13BA97F5A.png" width="110" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>)
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
2
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
S
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6DD17BD892E7CB9754C8FAFEEA4960A2"><img src="../media/6DD17BD892E7CB9754C8FAFEEA4960A2.png" width="7" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — relative shear modulus of network B
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
3
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
E
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
Abaqus regularization constant, plays the role of <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#75064C27E3D052F8F27E8C520763AE64"><img src="../media/75064C27E3D052F8F27E8C520763AE64.png" width="7" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
4
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
C
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
strain exponent in the flow law
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
5
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
A
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
raw Abaqus creep constant, units <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6A32C1D50F2988B982C65C5F6D3CC356"><img src="../media/6A32C1D50F2988B982C65C5F6D3CC356.png" width="69" height="17" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</entry></row>

<row>
<entry>
<para>
6
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
M
</para>
</entry><entry>
<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2C3A6651FE3DC1F1594E7ADEC315B14B"><img src="../media/2C3A6651FE3DC1F1594E7ADEC315B14B.png" width="13" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> — stress exponent in the flow law
</para>
</entry></row>
</table>

<para>
Converted internally as: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2CF458DF5B198281043B0DDCCB7D20D7"><img src="../media/2CF458DF5B198281043B0DDCCB7D20D7.png" width="63" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#68A15A6F1396630C9586639210BCD88A"><img src="../media/68A15A6F1396630C9586639210BCD88A.png" width="63" height="17" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B9003B0BD8FCC1910D116087AECC018E"><img src="../media/B9003B0BD8FCC1910D116087AECC018E.png" width="38" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0A9BD6B9B61E2F39447B09BBD9CC7A9B"><img src="../media/0A9BD6B9B61E2F39447B09BBD9CC7A9B.png" width="67" height="17" align="texttop"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B40D5833D4CEBD09B7BE95DF99CC3974"><img src="../media/B40D5833D4CEBD09B7BE95DF99CC3974.png" width="49" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
You can also choose between engineering stress as dependent variable, or the Cauchy stress by setting the property <codeInline>DependentVariableIsCauchyStress</codeInline>. Note that when this variable is set to <codeInline>true</codeInline>, the property <codeInline>CrossSectionArea</codeInline> has to be set to <codeInline>1</codeInline>. On the other hand, if property <codeInline>DependentVariableIsCauchyStress</codeInline> is set to false, the dependent variable would be the engineering stress (if <codeInline>CrossSectionArea</codeInline> is set to <codeInline>1</codeInline>) or the force (if <codeInline>CrossSectionArea</codeInline> is set to the initial cross section of the sample.
</para>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
J. S. Bergström and M. C. Boyce, “Constitutive modeling of the large strain time-dependent behavior of elastomers,” Journal of the Mechanics and Physics of Solids, vol. 46, no. 5, pp. 931–954, May 1998, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.1016/S0022-5096(97)00075-6</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-5096(97)00075-6</linkUri></externalLink>.
.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
E. M. Arruda and M. C. Boyce, “A three-dimensional constitutive model for the large stretch behavior of rubber elastic materials,” Journal of the Mechanics and Physics of Solids, vol. 41, no. 2, pp. 389–412, Feb. 1993, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.1016/0022-5096(93)90013-6</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-5096(93)90013-6</linkUri></externalLink>.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
J. S. Bergström and M. C. Boyce, “Constitutive modeling of the time-dependent and cyclic loading of elastomers and application to soft biological tissues,” Mechanics of Materials, vol. 33, no. 9, pp. 523–530, Sep. 2001, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.1016/S0167-6636(01)00070-9</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.1016/S0167-6636(01)00070-9</linkUri></externalLink>.
.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="2E5BB36E101190340701AF9BE2B3F1DD">
<title>Extended tube model (equibiaxial load)
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the equibiaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the four material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF"><img src="../media/FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE"><img src="../media/A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE.png" width="7" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413"><img src="../media/AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34"><img src="../media/4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34.png" width="8" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F21A57186012D3B284DDC9DE6D857037"><img src="../media/F21A57186012D3B284DDC9DE6D857037.png" width="460" height="41"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF"><img src="../media/FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : modulus parameter (cross-link contribution, in units of the dependent variable)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE"><img src="../media/A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE.png" width="7" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : material parameter (unitless, 0..1)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413"><img src="../media/AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : modulus parameter (constraint contribution, in units of the dependent variable)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34"><img src="../media/4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34.png" width="8" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : material parameter (unitless)
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0B2B2985FFEB853AD72A4110B4838A6A"><img src="../media/0B2B2985FFEB853AD72A4110B4838A6A.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's equibiaxial data ([2], black circles), and the extended tube model (green line) with the parameters ([1], Table 1, No 1): <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F0DEF7ECAE72DAAE078D65774B41AFB8"><img src="../media/F0DEF7ECAE72DAAE078D65774B41AFB8.png" width="99" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4A899274B05BA68D31173DC51E010D4E"><img src="../media/4A899274B05BA68D31173DC51E010D4E.png" width="76" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1AA9695FEF67D64E94BF26ADD376E5C8"><img src="../media/1AA9695FEF67D64E94BF26ADD376E5C8.png" width="100" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B51E99B6741F7C343340DC5358C3A2E9"><img src="../media/B51E99B6741F7C343340DC5358C3A2E9.png" width="48" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2A01E1EAA0A9F81BCD9046CF36F76B93"><img src="../media/2A01E1EAA0A9F81BCD9046CF36F76B93.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial data [2], and a fit with the extended tube models for equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial load simultaneously to all three data sets using the same parameters. The resulting fit parameters are <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3D4C9698D8AB226395EC31F37997ACC8"><img src="../media/3D4C9698D8AB226395EC31F37997ACC8.png" width="111" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#029039F43819CAD90D6C025B9839A81A"><img src="../media/029039F43819CAD90D6C025B9839A81A.png" width="83" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1F7DA36F039160AF4EA1582B00C94209"><img src="../media/1F7DA36F039160AF4EA1582B00C94209.png" width="100" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#007620FC22966A3E09F0EF941C925484"><img src="../media/007620FC22966A3E09F0EF941C925484.png" width="85" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
[1] M. Kaliske and G. Heinrich, “An Extended Tube-Model for Rubber Elasticity: Statistical-Mechanical Theory and Finite Element Implementation,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 72, no. 4, pp. 602–632, Sep. 1999, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.5254/1.3538822</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.5254/1.3538822</linkUri></externalLink>.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
[2] L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.1039/tf9444000059</linkUri></externalLink>.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="E42EE411CA6354A5D99B5F26B0397901">
<title>Extended tube model (planar load)
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the planar stretching (width&gt;&gt;length) of an elastomeric material using the four material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF"><img src="../media/FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE"><img src="../media/A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE.png" width="7" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413"><img src="../media/AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34"><img src="../media/4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34.png" width="8" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9999E921A19A50253B774913B139B58B"><img src="../media/9999E921A19A50253B774913B139B58B.png" width="418" height="41"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF"><img src="../media/FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : modulus parameter (cross-link contribution, in units of the dependent variable)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE"><img src="../media/A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE.png" width="7" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : material parameter (unitless, 0..1)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413"><img src="../media/AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : modulus parameter (constraint contribution, in units of the dependent variable)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34"><img src="../media/4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34.png" width="8" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : material parameter (unitless)
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1F3A65ED5655A7A54A594E278F1E66BB"><img src="../media/1F3A65ED5655A7A54A594E278F1E66BB.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's pure shear data ([2], black circles), and the extended tube model (green line) with the parameters ([1], Table 1, No 1): <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F0DEF7ECAE72DAAE078D65774B41AFB8"><img src="../media/F0DEF7ECAE72DAAE078D65774B41AFB8.png" width="99" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4A899274B05BA68D31173DC51E010D4E"><img src="../media/4A899274B05BA68D31173DC51E010D4E.png" width="76" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1AA9695FEF67D64E94BF26ADD376E5C8"><img src="../media/1AA9695FEF67D64E94BF26ADD376E5C8.png" width="100" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B51E99B6741F7C343340DC5358C3A2E9"><img src="../media/B51E99B6741F7C343340DC5358C3A2E9.png" width="48" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
[1] M. Kaliske and G. Heinrich, “An Extended Tube-Model for Rubber Elasticity: Statistical-Mechanical Theory and Finite Element Implementation,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 72, no. 4, pp. 602–632, Sep. 1999, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.5254/1.3538822</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.5254/1.3538822</linkUri></externalLink>.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
[2] L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.1039/tf9444000059</linkUri></externalLink>.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="F35D1E5B0C375AFFA3F115D900486442">
<title>Extended tube model (uniaxial load)
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the uniaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the four material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF"><img src="../media/FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE"><img src="../media/A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE.png" width="7" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413"><img src="../media/AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34"><img src="../media/4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34.png" width="8" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#61446E71DE77184DC7035B15CA5F4E1B"><img src="../media/61446E71DE77184DC7035B15CA5F4E1B.png" width="446" height="42"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF"><img src="../media/FFB3482303E3462BD3D846553A69CDAF.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : modulus parameter (cross-link contribution, in units of the dependent variable)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE"><img src="../media/A0A2C470358E481F088A0FBF8D843BEE.png" width="7" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : material parameter (unitless, 0..1)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413"><img src="../media/AFDC6EFF12D958252DCAFC5250E09413.png" width="17" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : modulus parameter (constraint contribution, in units of the dependent variable)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34"><img src="../media/4529DD909963469A738B1430F79E7F34.png" width="8" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : material parameter (unitless)
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#041E325E6BA7E031A0A2CDDCC7F63DB5"><img src="../media/041E325E6BA7E031A0A2CDDCC7F63DB5.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's uniaxial data ([2], black circles), and the extended tube model (green line) with the parameters ([1], Table 1, No 1): <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F0DEF7ECAE72DAAE078D65774B41AFB8"><img src="../media/F0DEF7ECAE72DAAE078D65774B41AFB8.png" width="99" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4A899274B05BA68D31173DC51E010D4E"><img src="../media/4A899274B05BA68D31173DC51E010D4E.png" width="76" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1AA9695FEF67D64E94BF26ADD376E5C8"><img src="../media/1AA9695FEF67D64E94BF26ADD376E5C8.png" width="100" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B51E99B6741F7C343340DC5358C3A2E9"><img src="../media/B51E99B6741F7C343340DC5358C3A2E9.png" width="48" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
[1] M. Kaliske and G. Heinrich, “An Extended Tube-Model for Rubber Elasticity: Statistical-Mechanical Theory and Finite Element Implementation,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 72, no. 4, pp. 602–632, Sep. 1999, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.5254/1.3538822</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.5254/1.3538822</linkUri></externalLink>.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
[2] L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, <externalLink><linkText>doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059</linkText><linkUri>https://doi.org/10.1039/tf9444000059</linkUri></externalLink>.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="BE8D3F67113FF52C85FD8F789AB0035A">
<title>GentBiaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the equibiaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9AD27F1C3A166CC3BD902A0B08AACC48"><img src="../media/9AD27F1C3A166CC3BD902A0B08AACC48.png" width="169" height="38"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : shear modulus
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : maximum value of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1881E15CE2BB90E92F1B0A5739141D98"><img src="../media/1881E15CE2BB90E92F1B0A5739141D98.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the GentBiaxial model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AA74F6B1F9C89E1E2080D53D44601E31"><img src="../media/AA74F6B1F9C89E1E2080D53D44601E31.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E83B5D4CF5A1D7F4D6DC603B8C7CCEAA"><img src="../media/E83B5D4CF5A1D7F4D6DC603B8C7CCEAA.png" width="83" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3E2DAF7C87756C314B5E3AF284FE5E87"><img src="../media/3E2DAF7C87756C314B5E3AF284FE5E87.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial data [2], and a fit with the Gent models for equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial load simultaneously to all three data sets using the same parameters. The resulting fit parameters are <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A4FE331A3ACDA021811AE25CA3CB25B8"><img src="../media/A4FE331A3ACDA021811AE25CA3CB25B8.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#8A6DA9A66242D234CE7204544E25EED2"><img src="../media/8A6DA9A66242D234CE7204544E25EED2.png" width="80" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
A. N. Gent, “A New Constitutive Relation for Rubber,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 69, no. 1, pp. 59–61, Mar. 1996, doi: 10.5254/1.3538357.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="F09925CD34C714FB48383B974BDBA77B">
<title>GentPlanar model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the planar stretching (width&gt;&gt;length) of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5257C5147109E9294D6FAE366E47144D"><img src="../media/5257C5147109E9294D6FAE366E47144D.png" width="149" height="41"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : shear modulus
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : maximum value of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79574A1E4DF703E97E47892B1982929F"><img src="../media/79574A1E4DF703E97E47892B1982929F.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's pure shear data (black circles) fitted with the GentPlanar model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#64CABC3A3215BE9F9D8879AA846DEBFC"><img src="../media/64CABC3A3215BE9F9D8879AA846DEBFC.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7F9E07A28AD53BF688E0CDD76D78B787"><img src="../media/7F9E07A28AD53BF688E0CDD76D78B787.png" width="64" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
A. N. Gent, “A New Constitutive Relation for Rubber,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 69, no. 1, pp. 59–61, Mar. 1996, doi: 10.5254/1.3538357.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="2A249F516F283591532374502BCD5230">
<title>GentUniaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the uniaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#44F6F90B9758CDCC393F410B90E089EC"><img src="../media/44F6F90B9758CDCC393F410B90E089EC.png" width="169" height="41"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621"><img src="../media/4CE90C12D81643A2D3164C5F6410B621.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : shear modulus
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D"><img src="../media/001934C0B284A08FDEBB5E5707E9B91D.png" width="21" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : maximum value of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FD135AE8C43ACD590F9117D752775F5"><img src="../media/9FD135AE8C43ACD590F9117D752775F5.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's uniaxial data (black circles) fitted with the GentUniaxial model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#13462CBB0C4BF790CE2D7425532F3EF4"><img src="../media/13462CBB0C4BF790CE2D7425532F3EF4.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9E343E12A1348E4F0E15BD8EA42B1653"><img src="../media/9E343E12A1348E4F0E15BD8EA42B1653.png" width="68" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
A. N. Gent, “A New Constitutive Relation for Rubber,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 69, no. 1, pp. 59–61, Mar. 1996, doi: 10.5254/1.3538357.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="6F25714B1DA71A3D7FFFF498233C1C09">
<title>MooneyRivlinBiaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the biaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367"><img src="../media/7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BD71E9D6D60F78AF277498F57B64EA5F"><img src="../media/BD71E9D6D60F78AF277498F57B64EA5F.png" width="184" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367"><img src="../media/7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the second invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7008F1FC29CFAD67DD7B08100D6BF704"><img src="../media/7008F1FC29CFAD67DD7B08100D6BF704.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BFC9A56CCD5C5B153F17270794BBD50A"><img src="../media/BFC9A56CCD5C5B153F17270794BBD50A.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the MooneyRivlinBiaxial model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#1940587662D5BB4D05845A2A9BD15DE3"><img src="../media/1940587662D5BB4D05845A2A9BD15DE3.png" width="106" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#32602822CC7BE8457AA7CFAF7ABD2A45"><img src="../media/32602822CC7BE8457AA7CFAF7ABD2A45.png" width="108" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#66046CA935A4E1F0B4CA971B3533887C"><img src="../media/66046CA935A4E1F0B4CA971B3533887C.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial data [2], and a fit with the Mooney-Rivlin models for equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial load simultaneously to all three data sets using the same parameters. The resulting fit parameter is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AA1B471FE868BF1C64A6C9819C3E3865"><img src="../media/AA1B471FE868BF1C64A6C9819C3E3865.png" width="108" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#CC8245BF40DE162B28641239981BA3A5"><img src="../media/CC8245BF40DE162B28641239981BA3A5.png" width="124" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
M. Mooney, “A Theory of Large Elastic Deformation,” Journal of Applied Physics, vol. 11, no. 9, pp. 582–592, Sep. 1940, doi: 10.1063/1.1712836.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
R. S. Rivlin, “Large elastic deformations of isotropic materials IV. further developments of the general theory,” Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London. Series A, Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 241, no. 835, pp. 379–397, Oct. 1948, doi: 10.1098/rsta.1948.0024.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
M. C. Boyce and E. M. Arruda, “Constitutive Models of Rubber Elasticity: A Review,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 73, no. 3, pp. 504–523, Jul. 2000, doi: 10.5254/1.3547602.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="54E959AAED200A0EEE9CB4C3F03FE9FD">
<title>MooneyRivlinPlanar model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the planar stretching (width&gt;&gt;length)  of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367"><img src="../media/7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#824B09212591781FD8A00BCB6E1B0182"><img src="../media/824B09212591781FD8A00BCB6E1B0182.png" width="160" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367"><img src="../media/7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the second invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7008F1FC29CFAD67DD7B08100D6BF704"><img src="../media/7008F1FC29CFAD67DD7B08100D6BF704.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
ATTENTION: as can be seen from the formula above, a determination of both <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367"><img src="../media/7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> is not possible from a planar experiment as both parameters are covariant. Thus, in a fit, you have to set one of the parameters to a fixed value, which was determined by another experiment (e.g., a uniaxial experiment).
</para>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9BCAD53B9F5550FF5D27192242DBA22C"><img src="../media/9BCAD53B9F5550FF5D27192242DBA22C.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the MooneyRivlinPlanar model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3F2E65A1BE0E96EF7BA124C69843DE5F"><img src="../media/3F2E65A1BE0E96EF7BA124C69843DE5F.png" width="108" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> (fixed);  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#08D5E72B61B0CE8ED12F7E981BF73583"><img src="../media/08D5E72B61B0CE8ED12F7E981BF73583.png" width="101" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
M. Mooney, “A Theory of Large Elastic Deformation,” Journal of Applied Physics, vol. 11, no. 9, pp. 582–592, Sep. 1940, doi: 10.1063/1.1712836.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
R. S. Rivlin, “Large elastic deformations of isotropic materials IV. further developments of the general theory,” Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London. Series A, Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 241, no. 835, pp. 379–397, Oct. 1948, doi: 10.1098/rsta.1948.0024.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
M. C. Boyce and E. M. Arruda, “Constitutive Models of Rubber Elasticity: A Review,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 73, no. 3, pp. 504–523, Jul. 2000, doi: 10.5254/1.3547602.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="BDE664394F369F5C5CE66321F1806945">
<title>MooneyRivlinUniaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the uniaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the two material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367"><img src="../media/7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D51DB33C69474CBDF61E428BED70115B"><img src="../media/D51DB33C69474CBDF61E428BED70115B.png" width="169" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367"><img src="../media/7A9753B56475E02384627EAF5B716367.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the second invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7008F1FC29CFAD67DD7B08100D6BF704"><img src="../media/7008F1FC29CFAD67DD7B08100D6BF704.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9C560017FB8925F3E3E192EC41D4EB92"><img src="../media/9C560017FB8925F3E3E192EC41D4EB92.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the MooneyRivlinUniaxial model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7C8235B44DD9170CFEA16E09CB86C5B6"><img src="../media/7C8235B44DD9170CFEA16E09CB86C5B6.png" width="108" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>;  <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#07616BF2956CD69AD99BDF9B10943149"><img src="../media/07616BF2956CD69AD99BDF9B10943149.png" width="71" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> (constrained by <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A8498CB03064D1F84EAE8478C778CA8C"><img src="../media/A8498CB03064D1F84EAE8478C778CA8C.png" width="53" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>).
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
M. Mooney, “A Theory of Large Elastic Deformation,” Journal of Applied Physics, vol. 11, no. 9, pp. 582–592, Sep. 1940, doi: 10.1063/1.1712836.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
R. S. Rivlin, “Large elastic deformations of isotropic materials IV. further developments of the general theory,” Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London. Series A, Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 241, no. 835, pp. 379–397, Oct. 1948, doi: 10.1098/rsta.1948.0024.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
M. C. Boyce and E. M. Arruda, “Constitutive Models of Rubber Elasticity: A Review,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 73, no. 3, pp. 504–523, Jul. 2000, doi: 10.5254/1.3547602.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="6AE606643CD89A1A556A4A0D9DB64FBA">
<title>NeoHookeanBiaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the equibiaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the material parameter <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A60AD3EAEECBFB8892142A67394BCD3E"><img src="../media/A60AD3EAEECBFB8892142A67394BCD3E.png" width="125" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3CD15178DE06C9E50DF10C8868EFB585"><img src="../media/3CD15178DE06C9E50DF10C8868EFB585.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's equibiaxial data (black circles) fitted with the NeoHookeanBiaxial model (green line). The resulting parameter is: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3F2F2EAD01EAC7C681FE547C4EBD8D38"><img src="../media/3F2F2EAD01EAC7C681FE547C4EBD8D38.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#833DB7904A192EF5EEAED2C7C308FC49"><img src="../media/833DB7904A192EF5EEAED2C7C308FC49.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial data [2], and a fit with the Neo-Hookean models for equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial load simultaneously to all three data sets using the same parameters. The resulting fit parameter is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#8E187FB8C3EF8EBF2F2CA7C338E5FA75"><img src="../media/8E187FB8C3EF8EBF2F2CA7C338E5FA75.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
R. S. Rivlin, “Large elastic deformations of isotropic materials IV. further developments of the general theory,” Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London. Series A, Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 241, no. 835, pp. 379–397, Oct. 1948, doi: 10.1098/rsta.1948.0024.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="1F1053A951FCAE7D9C07877BF1F9A05B">
<title>NeoHookeanPlanar model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the planar stretching (width&gt;&gt;length)  of an elastomeric material using the material parameter <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0BAC911414BF68A411BA616B0CD57B92"><img src="../media/0BAC911414BF68A411BA616B0CD57B92.png" width="125" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9BCAD53B9F5550FF5D27192242DBA22C"><img src="../media/9BCAD53B9F5550FF5D27192242DBA22C.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the NeoHookeanPlanar model (green line). The resulting parameter is: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#8B8118350E2CCCE98D829EDB030075BE"><img src="../media/8B8118350E2CCCE98D829EDB030075BE.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
R. S. Rivlin, “Large elastic deformations of isotropic materials IV. further developments of the general theory,” Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London. Series A, Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 241, no. 835, pp. 379–397, Oct. 1948, doi: 10.1098/rsta.1948.0024.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="3D73D88339DE129C7BED9AC3999C15C2">
<title>NeoHookeanUniaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the uniaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the material parameter <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4F3B3646BF6A316EB076FD3E5859DCEC"><img src="../media/4F3B3646BF6A316EB076FD3E5859DCEC.png" width="125" height="37"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D9DFC6AD8DF982286BBD91866914652D"><img src="../media/D9DFC6AD8DF982286BBD91866914652D.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the NeoHookeanUniaxial model (green line). The resulting parameter is: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#03CF1722B14AA08F10314C0084C41446"><img src="../media/03CF1722B14AA08F10314C0084C41446.png" width="94" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
R. S. Rivlin, “Large elastic deformations of isotropic materials IV. further developments of the general theory,” Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London. Series A, Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 241, no. 835, pp. 379–397, Oct. 1948, doi: 10.1098/rsta.1948.0024.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="1B9178AF246FF76538E52BE7F6DCBE83">
<title>OdgenBiaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the biaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4"><img src="../media/E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975"><img src="../media/5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975.png" width="15" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#83557A973D75DD719082BAC08DB1CC7D"><img src="../media/83557A973D75DD719082BAC08DB1CC7D.png" width="180" height="51"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4"><img src="../media/E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> : prefactor of term <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548"><img src="../media/5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548.png" width="8" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975"><img src="../media/5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975.png" width="15" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> : exponent of term <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548"><img src="../media/5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548.png" width="8" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
The number of terms <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BEB797C1973E4A73B1655AE00803CA01"><img src="../media/BEB797C1973E4A73B1655AE00803CA01.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> can be configured by double-clicking on the fit function. Default value is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF"><img src="../media/D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7A47477241D9E6E6A20951B8B95E134A"><img src="../media/7A47477241D9E6E6A20951B8B95E134A.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the OdgenBiaxial model (green line) with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF"><img src="../media/D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>. The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F6F1677FC1CB0DAE8E34BDB5CBC8744E"><img src="../media/F6F1677FC1CB0DAE8E34BDB5CBC8744E.png" width="97" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A62B82A8053498D9ACCC6277B5ACD78E"><img src="../media/A62B82A8053498D9ACCC6277B5ACD78E.png" width="77" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#26A378D566522393E0CDDFB9643E160F"><img src="../media/26A378D566522393E0CDDFB9643E160F.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the OdgenBiaxial model (green line) with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B10881C2C6EC9181BCEEB41E8243EF89"><img src="../media/B10881C2C6EC9181BCEEB41E8243EF89.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>. The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#C0E864AA95BA84CCD346CA658E0D2CFA"><img src="../media/C0E864AA95BA84CCD346CA658E0D2CFA.png" width="94" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#31A6FA4E23C380286371672263C4674A"><img src="../media/31A6FA4E23C380286371672263C4674A.png" width="69" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#73885912D8F099BA7E1D3F6DE58ECC9D"><img src="../media/73885912D8F099BA7E1D3F6DE58ECC9D.png" width="97" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#32375CE105275D97BAD1F1F880EF5F6A"><img src="../media/32375CE105275D97BAD1F1F880EF5F6A.png" width="77" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4B3A2AB1A15151881E09415C4A71C9A8"><img src="../media/4B3A2AB1A15151881E09415C4A71C9A8.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial data [2], and a fit with the Odgen models for equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial load simultaneously to all three data sets using the same parameters. The resulting fit parameter are <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#11A3171D086919640576FC6DA02BFE4F"><img src="../media/11A3171D086919640576FC6DA02BFE4F.png" width="139" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#65D2077A8A35A95A6A7C6759BEC0D7BB"><img src="../media/65D2077A8A35A95A6A7C6759BEC0D7BB.png" width="62" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D73456D5D3BC84DCB6A40857C7E77B1C"><img src="../media/D73456D5D3BC84DCB6A40857C7E77B1C.png" width="124" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E9F72079B8B5DAC5DAB7C737E2EA89B5"><img src="../media/E9F72079B8B5DAC5DAB7C737E2EA89B5.png" width="77" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
R. W. Ogden, “Large deformation isotropic elasticity – on the correlation of theory and experiment for incompressible rubberlike solids,” Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. A. Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 326, no. 1567, pp. 565–584, Feb. 1972, doi: 10.1098/rspa.1972.0026.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="8AB90C6EE9AD23FFD509D999EBA7EF10">
<title>OdgenPlanar model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the planar stretching (width&gt;&gt;length) of an elastomeric material using the material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4"><img src="../media/E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975"><img src="../media/5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975.png" width="15" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6CE449EDE5F9B913CC21A7EB2D8D4611"><img src="../media/6CE449EDE5F9B913CC21A7EB2D8D4611.png" width="175" height="51"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4"><img src="../media/E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> : prefactor of term <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548"><img src="../media/5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548.png" width="8" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975"><img src="../media/5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975.png" width="15" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> : exponent of term <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548"><img src="../media/5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548.png" width="8" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
The number of terms <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BEB797C1973E4A73B1655AE00803CA01"><img src="../media/BEB797C1973E4A73B1655AE00803CA01.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> can be configured by double-clicking on the fit function. Default value is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF"><img src="../media/D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#235665EB33AEBCD499784D1189A8C9A3"><img src="../media/235665EB33AEBCD499784D1189A8C9A3.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's pure shear data (black circles) fitted with the OdgenPlanar model (green line) with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF"><img src="../media/D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>. The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#81479046AEB4A16039DCF54A0D040B55"><img src="../media/81479046AEB4A16039DCF54A0D040B55.png" width="97" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F84D4F2B427A1338E9B664A2C85BFEEE"><img src="../media/F84D4F2B427A1338E9B664A2C85BFEEE.png" width="77" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#675D8ED2D5568A9193AAC8EDB564CFBA"><img src="../media/675D8ED2D5568A9193AAC8EDB564CFBA.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's pure shear data (black circles) fitted with the OdgenPlanar model (green line) with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B10881C2C6EC9181BCEEB41E8243EF89"><img src="../media/B10881C2C6EC9181BCEEB41E8243EF89.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>. The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#784310449AFAD3D3EC721F3FA4FFBD94"><img src="../media/784310449AFAD3D3EC721F3FA4FFBD94.png" width="94" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#EDE559F535389F49C7EEAE358E27223B"><img src="../media/EDE559F535389F49C7EEAE358E27223B.png" width="69" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#C150E46E8B41886204CF9C1A2DF49AC1"><img src="../media/C150E46E8B41886204CF9C1A2DF49AC1.png" width="97" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#96362CAAD6EA91A63338FB3BAEA11B92"><img src="../media/96362CAAD6EA91A63338FB3BAEA11B92.png" width="69" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
R. W. Ogden, “Large deformation isotropic elasticity – on the correlation of theory and experiment for incompressible rubberlike solids,” Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. A. Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 326, no. 1567, pp. 565–584, Feb. 1972, doi: 10.1098/rspa.1972.0026.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="F251C81F2C8C83A0EAC61ACD02C59942">
<title>OdgenUniaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the uniaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4"><img src="../media/E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975"><img src="../media/5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975.png" width="15" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#CE56A3A77FCE384E8F9E3A02A0D5C36E"><img src="../media/CE56A3A77FCE384E8F9E3A02A0D5C36E.png" width="186" height="51"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4"><img src="../media/E82AF399DF44A1C24CE2AD7A94FB64C4.png" width="15" height="10" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> : prefactor of term <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548"><img src="../media/5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548.png" width="8" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975"><img src="../media/5854A25420AFF156D5A1A8581F0C9975.png" width="15" height="11" align="bottom"></img></a></markup> : exponent of term <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548"><img src="../media/5868D51818052F8A97B5E2A2D2D92548.png" width="8" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
The number of terms <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BEB797C1973E4A73B1655AE00803CA01"><img src="../media/BEB797C1973E4A73B1655AE00803CA01.png" width="12" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> can be configured by double-clicking on the fit function. Default value is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF"><img src="../media/D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4402002FCC95FBE354A9BC382238FDF8"><img src="../media/4402002FCC95FBE354A9BC382238FDF8.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's uniaxial data (black circles) fitted with the OdgenUniaxial model (green line) with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF"><img src="../media/D4A484E6F95897CED55444C8A7EBE2DF.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>. The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BE862E22F34DB71FCFC7DBC25E39C770"><img src="../media/BE862E22F34DB71FCFC7DBC25E39C770.png" width="101" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#07663F7583D21E627252FAF40B893F3E"><img src="../media/07663F7583D21E627252FAF40B893F3E.png" width="69" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AD4F740CFA51996EED8015CA0DDBD772"><img src="../media/AD4F740CFA51996EED8015CA0DDBD772.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's uniaxial data (black circles) fitted with the OdgenUniaxial model (green line) with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#B10881C2C6EC9181BCEEB41E8243EF89"><img src="../media/B10881C2C6EC9181BCEEB41E8243EF89.png" width="40" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>. The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A15F160B23DCE54F306110BC546F0E35"><img src="../media/A15F160B23DCE54F306110BC546F0E35.png" width="101" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#6C28656678A6B0D98F391763A208EEA6"><img src="../media/6C28656678A6B0D98F391763A208EEA6.png" width="92" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#80468B1705B68AA679790EECC77411C3"><img src="../media/80468B1705B68AA679790EECC77411C3.png" width="97" height="14" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D188C63F26ABB490CBDBA3587104D60F"><img src="../media/D188C63F26ABB490CBDBA3587104D60F.png" width="77" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
R. W. Ogden, “Large deformation isotropic elasticity – on the correlation of theory and experiment for incompressible rubberlike solids,” Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. A. Mathematical and Physical Sciences, vol. 326, no. 1567, pp. 565–584, Feb. 1972, doi: 10.1098/rspa.1972.0026.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="64BB16507E7A973C4D7DEFA6A50C928A">
<title>YeohBiaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the biaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the three material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58"><img src="../media/5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087"><img src="../media/0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#AE357E5E53D334DCBBF642FFA0FDBBCD"><img src="../media/AE357E5E53D334DCBBF642FFA0FDBBCD.png" width="519" height="40"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58"><img src="../media/5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : second order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087"><img src="../media/0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : third order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#D455A3A2EB6C732EC85FDB6874F4075F"><img src="../media/D455A3A2EB6C732EC85FDB6874F4075F.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's data (black circles) fitted with the YeohBiaxial model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3FC0677096A36080B0BC70B503D15DF2"><img src="../media/3FC0677096A36080B0BC70B503D15DF2.png" width="127" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#E8D6E5C338190B9D5018BDCC79DDBC67"><img src="../media/E8D6E5C338190B9D5018BDCC79DDBC67.png" width="117" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3C9798844A8373A306B41C56F47F42C1"><img src="../media/3C9798844A8373A306B41C56F47F42C1.png" width="105" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#3AD4419E08C938771C5642B407DA3893"><img src="../media/3AD4419E08C938771C5642B407DA3893.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 2: Treloar's equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial data [2], and a fit with the Yeoh models for equibiaxial, planar and uniaxial load simultaneously to all three data sets using the same parameters. The resulting fit parameter is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#7BDE501596156EC155525A2DAECF1492"><img src="../media/7BDE501596156EC155525A2DAECF1492.png" width="112" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#401289526E42ABA83E6D97F390E99406"><img src="../media/401289526E42ABA83E6D97F390E99406.png" width="132" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#A62E262920D1B37021D10A11A578A0C5"><img src="../media/A62E262920D1B37021D10A11A578A0C5.png" width="112" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
O. H. Yeoh, “Some Forms of the Strain Energy Function for Rubber,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 66, no. 5, pp. 754–771, Nov. 1993, doi: 10.5254/1.3538343.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="0D4FB924B126DF2FE6B69E70BC08EA20">
<title>YeohPlanar model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the planar stretching (width&gt;&gt;length) of an elastomeric material using the three material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58"><img src="../media/5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087"><img src="../media/0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#2381EC7AC0FCB2AAEFD1F534B6C01DE8"><img src="../media/2381EC7AC0FCB2AAEFD1F534B6C01DE8.png" width="381" height="40"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58"><img src="../media/5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : second order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087"><img src="../media/0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : third order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#ED66723465B79A4347517DF67B9CAE73"><img src="../media/ED66723465B79A4347517DF67B9CAE73.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's pure shear data (black circles) fitted with the YeohPlanar model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DD5F5E25495BFDB02C37D7EF1989B098"><img src="../media/DD5F5E25495BFDB02C37D7EF1989B098.png" width="108" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F2F19B8E4B1F128FD781011CAFD639C7"><img src="../media/F2F19B8E4B1F128FD781011CAFD639C7.png" width="124" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#54415DF66DAD77E49FD906966EA34444"><img src="../media/54415DF66DAD77E49FD906966EA34444.png" width="105" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
<markup><hr/></markup>

<para>
References:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
O. H. Yeoh, “Some Forms of the Strain Energy Function for Rubber,” Rubber Chemistry and Technology, vol. 66, no. 5, pp. 754–771, Nov. 1993, doi: 10.5254/1.3538343.
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
L. R. G. Treloar, “Stress-strain data for vulcanised rubber under various types of deformation,” Trans. Faraday Soc., vol. 40, p. 59, 1944, doi: 10.1039/tf9444000059.
</para>
</listItem>
</list>
</content>
</section>

<section address="63029B955EE256428930F2258E4649BB">
<title>YeohUniaxial model
</title>
<content>

<para>
This model evaluates the engineering stress as a function of engineering strain during the uniaxial stretching of an elastomeric material using the three material parameters <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58"><img src="../media/5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087"><img src="../media/0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>  according to the following  equation:
</para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#CFC416F2EAF60C769A2876F8B3139236"><img src="../media/CFC416F2EAF60C769A2876F8B3139236.png" width="456" height="40"></img></a></markup>

<para>
with <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85"><img src="../media/79DBD2D673A9E5702BE79AA239C84A85.png" width="61" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
in which:
</para>

<list class="bullet">

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632"><img src="../media/56FE4FC5B2B733E4B01083EB93F11632.png" width="6" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering strain
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8"><img src="../media/DE5EA325656D857AFB5CA4E8DB46FCC8.png" width="9" height="8" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : engineering stress (force divided by the cross section in the stress-free state)
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279"><img src="../media/BA168AB8F2D0120F2FFC26E0D2C43279.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : first order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58"><img src="../media/5970E60954011FD14053C45047F49F58.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : second order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>

<listItem>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087"><img src="../media/0BCCB31F5A7A2FC20E48F82BFD3FC087.png" width="26" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> : third order coefficient of the first invariant <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C"><img src="../media/9FB99C324BF2940C3979E2F29230721C.png" width="14" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>
</para>
</listItem>
</list>

<para>
In the case that your dependent data is force instead of stress, you can enter the initial cross section area by double-clicking on the fit function.
</para>

<para>
The domain of the function is <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774"><img src="../media/12ECE0B92E15B9351BC719D87B3CF774.png" width="80" height="12" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>

<para>
<markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#013888CC616C8EE97F03714393E3B57C"><img src="../media/013888CC616C8EE97F03714393E3B57C.png" width="567"></img></a></markup>
</para>

<para>
Fig. 1: Treloar's uniaxial data (black circles) fitted with the YeohUniaxial model (green line). The resulting parameters are: <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#229266B8C9EE3C5981F7473B4AA5C603"><img src="../media/229266B8C9EE3C5981F7473B4AA5C603.png" width="108" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>, <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#4FC276A37C52746342CC256F1B098854"><img src="../media/4FC276A37C52746342CC256F1B098854.png" width="117" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup> and <markup><a href="ACAC6A80-7CE0-4CB9-B36C-B2FB6ACAB027.htm#F68CA7B2B773B840D4DFF5E00C485ED3"><img src="../media/F68CA7B2B773B840D4DFF5E00C485ED3.png" width="97" height="13" align="middle"></img></a></markup>.
</para>
